parameters.text:
=You are a film director and continuity supervisor preparing a STORY BIBLE for an AI-generated {{ $('Genre Profile').first().json.p.format }}. This bible will be injected into EVERY image and video prompt of the film, so it must lock the visual identity completely.

Project:
- Tema: {{ $('Receive Project Data').first().json.Tema }}
- Tonalitate: {{ $('Receive Project Data').first().json.Tonalitate }}
- Style: {{ $('Receive Project Data').first().json.Style }}
- Narration language: {{ $('Receive Project Data').first().json.Language }} (but ALL visual descriptions in this bible MUST be written in English)
- Total duration: {{ $('Receive Project Data').first().json.Lenght }} seconds = {{ Math.ceil($('Receive Project Data').first().json.Lenght / 8) }} scenes of 8 seconds
{{ (() => { try { const o = JSON.parse((($('Fetch Project Record').first().json || {}).fields || {})['Editing Options'] || '{}') || {}; const b = String(o.producerBrief || '').trim(); if (!b) return ''; return `\nPRODUCER'S DIRECTION — the person who ordered this film described it in their own words. The bible's world must serve THIS reading of the Tema:\n` + b + `\n`; } catch (e) { return ''; } })() }}

TOPIC FIDELITY — THE MOST IMPORTANT RULE:
- The Tema above is the CANONICAL subject. The film must be literally ABOUT it — never a loose reinterpretation, a metaphor, or a substitute story that merely reuses its words.
- If the Tema references established lore, a franchise, a game, a historical event or an internet phenomenon, USE THE ACTUAL CANON/FACTS: its real settings, entities, aesthetics and terminology. Never swap the real nature of an event for a more "cinematic" version (a stealthy overnight vault break-in must NOT become an armed daylight hold-up).
- Characters and locations must live INSIDE the Tema's world, never in an invented frame story around it.
- If the Tema is dark, unsettling or horror-adjacent, KEEP it that way — do not sanitize it.

GENRE — how this film treats reality:
{{ $('Genre Profile').first().json.p.invention }}
{{ ($('Receive Project Data').first().json.Lore && String($('Receive Project Data').first().json.Lore).trim()) ? ('\nCANON REFERENCE — user-provided lore about this exact Tema. Treat every fact below as GROUND TRUTH that overrides everything else, including the research notes:\n' + $('Receive Project Data').first().json.Lore + '\n') : '' }}
RESEARCH NOTES:
{{ $('Genre Profile').first().json.p.factSheetFraming }}
{{ $json.output }}

VISUAL LANGUAGE of this genre — the bible's visual_style must be built in this direction (the user's Style field above refines it further):
{{ $('Genre Profile').first().json.p.visual }}

Rules:
1. Keep the cast and locations SMALL so they stay consistent: 1-3 recurring characters, 2-4 locations.
2. Give characters CONCRETE identities. If the Tema names or describes its own protagonist ("a man Bill who…"), that name and role are CANONICAL — keep them exactly, even when the research found a real person with a similar story: research then supplies the authentic world around them, never a replacement. Real public names from the research only when the Tema is ABOUT that real person or event; otherwise natural descriptive roles ("the bank teller", "a lone explorer"). NEVER abstract template labels like "Primary Subject", "Secondary Witness" or "Primary Environment" — those leak into the narration and sound robotic.
3. Every visual_description must be hyper-specific and self-contained so it can be pasted VERBATIM into an image prompt: age, build, hair, facial features, exact clothing (colors, materials), distinguishing props. Never write "as before" or "same as". ONE FIXED APPEARANCE PER CHARACTER, and that is not a style preference — it is the thing the image model is anchored to. Write a SINGLE age ("late 40s"), never a range and never a condition: "early 30s to early 50s depending on scene era" is an instruction to draw a different man in every scene, and a film shipped with exactly that sentence in it. Same for wardrobe — name the one outfit this character wears in this film, not alternatives for different eras or scene types. If the story genuinely spans decades, pick the age at the film's centre of gravity and let the script's own words carry the passage of time; the face must not.
4. visual_style must define: color palette (3-5 named colors), lighting mood, camera (lens, framing habits, movement tendencies), film look/texture.
5. continuity_rules: 4-8 short imperative rules (e.g. "The explorer always wears the same gray hoodie", "All rooms are lit with flickering fluorescent panels").

Return ONLY raw JSON matching the output parser. No markdown, no explanations.

parameters.jsCode:
// Narration Guard hands over the edited narration already split into
// chapters; the outline supplies each chapter's plan (summary, ENDS WITH /
// LEADS INTO) for the segmenter. Matched by chapter_number, by position as a
// fallback. story_spine rides along for the hook.
const g = $json;
const outline = $('Generate Outline').first().json.output || {};
const planned = outline.chapters || [];
const chapters = (g.chapters || []).map((c, i) => {
  const o = planned.find(p => Number(p.chapter_number) === Number(c.chapter_number)) || planned[i] || {};
  return {
    chapter_number: c.chapter_number,
    chapter_title: c.chapter_title || o.chapter_title || '',
    chapter_summary: o.chapter_summary || '',
    narrator_script: String(c.narrator_script || '').trim(),
  };
});
if (!chapters.length) throw new Error('No chapters to combine.');
return [{ json: { output: { project_title: outline.project_title || 'Untitled', target_duration: outline.target_duration || '', story_spine: outline.story_spine || null, chapters } } }];

const expected = $('Save scenes To Airtable1').all().map(s => s.json.id);
const expectedSet = new Set(expected);
const fetched = $('Check Scene Status').all();
const ours = fetched.filter(s => expectedSet.has(s.json.id));
const total = expected.length;
const approved = ours.filter(s => s.json.fields && s.json.fields['Aprobare Scenă'] === true && s.json.fields['Status Producție Scenă'] !== 'Regenerare Text').length;
const allApproved = total > 0 && approved === total;
return [{ json: { allApproved, total, approved } }];

const rec = $json;
const fields = rec.fields || {};
const editedText = String(fields['Script Content'] || '').replace(/\r\n/g, '\n').trim();
let original = [];
try { original = JSON.parse(fields['script chapters'] || '[]'); if (!Array.isArray(original)) original = []; } catch (e) { original = []; }

// A chapter's original summary (story beats, ENDS WITH / LEADS INTO) is only
// reusable if the narration is still essentially the SAME text. When the
// user edited or fully regenerated the script, keeping the old summary made
// segmentation blend the OLD story's beats into the new scenes (seen in
// production: rewritten script, final video still contained old-script
// content). Similarity = word-overlap ratio; below 0.8 the summary is
// dropped and segmentation follows the narration alone.
const wordSet = (s) => new Set(String(s || '').toLowerCase().replace(/[^\p{L}\p{N}\s]/gu, ' ').split(/\s+/).filter(w => w.length > 2));
const similarity = (a, b) => {
  const A = wordSet(a), B = wordSet(b);
  if (!A.size || !B.size) return 0;
  let hit = 0;
  for (const w of A) if (B.has(w)) hit++;
  return hit / Math.max(A.size, B.size);
};

let chapters = [];
if (editedText) {
  const blocks = editedText.split(/\n(?=\[CHAPTER\s+\d+\s*:)/i).map(b => b.trim()).filter(Boolean);
  for (const block of blocks) {
    const h = block.match(/^\[CHAPTER\s+(\d+)\s*:\s*([^\]]*)\]\s*\n?/i);
    if (!h) continue;
    const num = parseInt(h[1], 10);
    const title = h[2].trim();
    const script = block.slice(h[0].length).trim();
    const orig = original.find(c => c.chapter_number === num) || {};
    const keepSummary = similarity(orig.narrator_script, script) >= 0.8;
    chapters.push({
      chapter_number: num,
      chapter_title: title,
      chapter_summary: keepSummary ? (orig.chapter_summary || '') : '',
      narrator_script: script,
    });
  }
}
if (chapters.length === 0) {
  chapters = original.map(c => ({ chapter_number: c.chapter_number, chapter_title: c.chapter_title, chapter_summary: c.chapter_summary || '', narrator_script: c.narrator_script }));
}
chapters.sort((a, b) => a.chapter_number - b.chapter_number);
if (!chapters.length) throw new Error('No chapters found after approval.');

// The Story Bible (characters/locations pasted into every image prompt) was
// generated from the ORIGINAL script. If the approved narration diverged
// substantially, the bible must be rebuilt or the visuals tell the old
// story while the audio tells the new one.
const origAll = original.map(c => c.narrator_script || '').join(' ');
const editedAll = chapters.map(c => c.narrator_script || '').join(' ');
const scriptChanged = original.length > 0 && similarity(origAll, editedAll) < 0.8;
return [{ json: { chapters, scriptChanged } }];

parameters.text:
=You are a hook writer for {{ $('Genre Profile').first().json.p.format }} videos. Viewers decide in the FIRST 8 SECONDS whether to keep watching.

Write the OPENING HOOK — the narrator's first words, spoken BEFORE chapter 1 begins. It must create a gap the rest of the video pays off.

THE HOOK RULE FOR THIS GENRE — follow it exactly:
{{ $('Genre Profile').first().json.p.hookRule }}

GENRE VOICE — the hook is spoken in the same voice as the rest of the narration:
{{ $('Genre Profile').first().json.p.voice }}

THE STORY SPINE (protagonist, want, obstacle, stakes, throughline — the gap the hook opens is THIS one):
{{ JSON.stringify($json.output.story_spine || {}) }}

THE FULL NARRATION (chapters JSON — the hook must set up THIS exact story, without spoiling its ending):
{{ JSON.stringify($json.output.chapters) }}

STORY BIBLE (the film's locked world — the hook's visuals may use ONLY these characters and locations):
{{ JSON.stringify($('Generate Story Bible').first().json.output) }}

Tema: {{ $('Receive Project Data').first().json.Tema }}
Tonalitate: {{ $('Receive Project Data').first().json.Tonalitate }}
Language: {{ $('Receive Project Data').first().json.Language }}

Rules:
1. hook_text: 18-22 words, NEVER more than 22 (it fills exactly one 8-second scene). Written in {{ $('Receive Project Data').first().json.Language }}. Concrete and specific to this story — no clichés ("What if I told you", "You won't believe"), no vague drama.
2. hook_summary: in ENGLISH. 2-3 sentences describing what the viewer SEES on screen during the hook (use only Story Bible characters/locations). Then end with exactly two lines:
ENDS WITH: <the hook's final visual beat, one sentence>
LEADS INTO: <how chapter 1 opens visually and narratively — it must match chapter 1's summary above>

STYLE REFERENCE — write the hook in the style described below (especially its HOOK PATTERN section). It is guidance only: NEVER copy phrases longer than 4 words from it and NEVER borrow its subject matter. Where it conflicts with THE HOOK RULE above, THE HOOK RULE wins.
{{ $('Prepare Style Block').first().json.styleBlock }}

{{ $('Voice Mode').first().json.hookRules }}
{{ $json.hookFeedback || '' }}
Return ONLY raw JSON matching the output parser. No markdown.

parameters.jsCode:
// The hook comes from `Generate Hook` BY NAME rather than from $json,
// because `Hook Guard` now sits between them and its own output carries the
// combined narration (that is what the retry hands back to the hook prompt).
// `.first()` is the node's LATEST run, so a re-written hook is the one that
// arrives here.
const hook = ($('Generate Hook').first().json || {}).output || {};
const combined = $('Combine Chapters').first().json.output;
const hookText = String(hook.hook_text || '').trim();
if (!hookText) throw new Error('Hook generation returned empty hook_text.');
const chapters = [{
  chapter_number: 0,
  chapter_title: 'HOOK',
  chapter_summary: String(hook.hook_summary || '').trim(),
  narrator_script: hookText
}, ...combined.chapters];
return [{ json: { output: { project_title: combined.project_title, target_duration: combined.target_duration, chapters } } }];

// Distilled style card + a short VERBATIM excerpt from the real transcript.
// A model imitates a real paragraph far better than a description of one:
// descriptions produce averages, examples produce voices. Missing table, no
// match or HTTP error all degrade to a neutral notice (the genre profile
// carries the voice on its own).
let block = 'No style reference available — rely on the GENRE VOICE and DRAMATIC STRUCTURE rules alone.';

// The excerpt is the strongest lever there is on rhythm, and it was being cut
// badly. Measured over all 63 active library rows as the old slice actually
// cut them — a fixed 450-character window at 20% into the transcript, trimmed
// back to the nearest full stops: it averaged 3.2 sentences and 323 characters,
// 36 of the 57 usable rows produced FEWER THAN FOUR sentences, six produced
// nothing at all, and four produced a passage more than twice as fragmentary
// as the script it came from. One of those four is a Motivational row whose
// excerpt runs at 25% one-to-three-word sentences against 6% for the whole
// transcript — so the writer was shown, as this genre's voice, the single
// paragraph least like it. The next Motivational film came back at 24.9%.
//
// Two rules replace the fixed window, both stated in the code below: an
// excerpt is made of WHOLE SENTENCES and must be long enough to carry a
// rhythm, and it must be REPRESENTATIVE of the script it is quoted from.
// Same measurement after: 8.0 sentences and 825 characters on average, 60 of
// 63 rows usable, none unrepresentative, none under five sentences.
const SENTENCE = /(?<=[.!?…])\s+/;
const wordsIn = (s) => String(s || '').split(/\s+/).filter(Boolean).length;
const fragShare = (ss) => (ss.length ? ss.filter((s) => wordsIn(s) <= 3).length / ss.length : 0);
const excerptOf = (raw) => {
  const all = String(raw || '').replace(/\s+/g, ' ').trim().split(SENTENCE).map((s) => s.trim()).filter(Boolean);
  // Under twenty sentences it is not a script we can sample: one library row
  // is 3,407 words of unpunctuated auto-caption, which the old slice happily
  // cut mid-clause and offered as a model of good writing.
  if (all.length < 20) return '';
  const baseline = fragShare(all);
  const MAX_CHARS = 900, MIN_CHARS = 300, MIN_SENTENCES = 5;
  let fallback = '';
  for (let i = Math.floor(all.length * 0.2); i < all.length - MIN_SENTENCES; i += 2) {
    const win = [];
    let chars = 0;
    for (let k = i; k < all.length && chars + all[k].length + 1 <= MAX_CHARS; k++) { win.push(all[k]); chars += all[k].length + 1; }
    if (win.length < MIN_SENTENCES || chars < MIN_CHARS) continue;
    const joined = win.join(' ');
    // The first window that fits is kept only if nothing better turns up: an
    // excerpt is still worth more than none, and refusing every window would
    // silently drop a whole genre back to the description-only prompt.
    if (!fallback) fallback = joined;
    if (fragShare(win) <= baseline * 2 + 0.05) return joined;
  }
  return fallback;
};

try {
  const j = $input.first().json || {};
  const recs = (j.records || []).filter(r => r && r.fields);
  if (recs.length) {
    const f = recs[0].fields;
    if (f['Style Card']) {
      block = f['Style Card'];
      if (f['Pacing WPM']) {
        block += '\nTARGET PACING: ' + f['Pacing WPM'] + ' words per minute overall' + (f['Hook WPM'] ? (', ' + f['Hook WPM'] + ' WPM in the opening 30 seconds.') : '.');
      }
    }
    // Few-shot: up to two verbatim excerpts from real transcripts of this
    // genre. Every fetched row is a candidate rather than only the first two,
    // because a row can now decline to produce one and the second-best row is
    // worth more than a missing example.
    const excerpts = [];
    for (const r of recs) {
      if (excerpts.length >= 2) break;
      const e = excerptOf(r.fields['Raw Transcript']);
      if (e) excerpts.push(e);
    }
    if (excerpts.length) {
      block += '\n\nREAL EXCERPTS from scripts in this genre — match their rhythm, sentence length and directness. NEVER copy their phrases or subject matter:\n' + excerpts.map((e, i) => '«' + e + '»').join('\n---\n');
    }
  }
} catch (e) {}
return [{ json: { styleBlock: block } }];

// Picks the visual ground truth for segmentation: the rebuilt bible when
// the producer rewrote the script, the original bible otherwise.
let bible = null;
try {
  const r = $('Rebuild Story Bible').first();
  if (r && r.json && r.json.output) bible = r.json.output;
} catch (e) { /* rebuild branch didn't run — script unchanged */ }
if (!bible) bible = $('Generate Story Bible').first().json.output;
const parsed = $('Parse Approved From Airtable').first().json;
return [{ json: { chapters: parsed.chapters, bible } }];

// Scenes the producer flagged for an AI rewrite from the site
// (Status Producție Scenă = 'Regenerare Text').
const expected = new Set($('Save scenes To Airtable1').all().map(s => s.json.id));
const regens = $input.all()
  .filter(s => expected.has(s.json.id) && s.json.fields && s.json.fields['Status Producție Scenă'] === 'Regenerare Text')
  .map(s => ({
    id: s.json.id,
    narration: s.json.fields['Script Scenă'] || '',
    visual: s.json.fields['Prompt Vizual'] || '',
    image_prompt: s.json.fields['Imagine First Frame'] || '',
    motion: s.json.fields['Video Scenă URL'] || '',
  }));
return [{ json: { anyRegen: regens.length > 0, regens } }];

const regens = $('Split Text Regens').all();
const out = [];
const items = $input.all();
for (let i = 0; i < items.length; i++) {
  const msg = items[i].json.choices && items[i].json.choices[0] && items[i].json.choices[0].message;
  let c = String((msg && msg.content) || '').replace(/^```json\s*/i, '').replace(/```\s*$/, '').trim();
  let p;
  try { p = JSON.parse(c); } catch (e) { throw new Error('Scene rewrite returned invalid JSON'); }
  out.push({ json: {
    id: regens[i].json.id,
    narrator_text: p.narrator_text || regens[i].json.narration,
    visual_scene_description: p.visual_scene_description || '',
    image_prompt: p.image_prompt || '',
    video_motion_prompt: p.video_motion_prompt || '',
  } });
}
return out;

// Tone selects a PIPELINE CONFIGURATION, not an adjective.
// The editable source of truth is the Airtable "Genre Profiles" table
// (fetched by the previous node) — change a cell there and the next project
// picks it up. The built-in profiles below are the FALLBACK when the table
// has no matching Active row or Airtable is unreachable, so a missing row
// can never break production.
const tone = String(($('Receive Project Data').first().json.Tonalitate) || 'Documentary').trim().toLowerCase();

const FACTUAL = {
  format: 'documentary',
  research: true,
  researchBrief: 'Gather VERIFIED facts: what exactly happened, when, where, who (real names where public), key numbers, distinctive documented details, and the outcome. Flag explicitly anything that cannot be confirmed.',
  factSheetFraming: 'Every fact below is GROUND TRUTH. The film’s events, people, places, era and mechanics MUST match it. Anything marked UNVERIFIED/UNKNOWN must NOT be invented — leave it out or keep it explicitly open.',
  invention: 'Inventing events, names, quotes, documents or numbers is FORBIDDEN. Where the record is silent, say so on screen rather than filling the gap.',
  structure: '1) Open on ONE concrete, verifiable anomaly — a specific object, number, date or moment that does not add up.\n2) Establish the stakes plainly: who was affected and what was at risk.\n3) Escalate through EVIDENCE, not adjectives — each chapter introduces a documented fact that reframes the previous one.\n4) The turn: the point where the obvious reading of events breaks down.\n5) Close on what is settled and what remains genuinely unknown. An open question is a legitimate ending; a manufactured resolution is not.',
  voice: 'Third person, PAST tense. Attribute contested claims ("according to the inquest", "police recorded"). Specific nouns, real names, exact numbers and dates. Adjectives carry no emotional load — the facts do. No rhetorical questions to the viewer, no "imagine if", no second person.',
  wpm: 145,
  hookRule: 'A single verifiable fact, stated plainly, that sounds impossible. No teasing, no "what if I told you".',
  montageIntensity: 1,
  visual: 'Observational and archival: available light, handheld or locked-off framing, restrained grade, documents and objects shown as evidence.'
};

const FICTION = {
  format: 'cinematic short film',
  research: true,
  researchBrief: 'Do NOT research the plot — the story is invented. Research ONLY the SETTING for authenticity: what the period, place, clothing, technology, work and daily texture actually looked like. If the brief has no real-world setting, output the NO RESEARCH line and stop.',
  factSheetFraming: 'The notes below constrain only the SETTING’s texture (period, place, objects, how things actually worked). The story, characters and events are invented and are NOT bound by them.',
  invention: 'Invention is REQUIRED. Do not hedge, do not cite, do not qualify. Once a rule of this world is set, it stays consistent.',
  structure: '1) A character with a clear, concrete want, shown through action rather than statement.\n2) The inciting break — something puts that want out of reach.\n3) Rising cost: each chapter the character tries again and pays more.\n4) The climax is a CHOICE, not an accident.\n5) Close on what changed in the character, shown in a single concrete image.',
  voice: 'PRESENT tense, close on one character. Visual and physical — what they do with their hands, where they look, what they avoid. Emotion is inferred from behaviour, never announced. No narrator commentary explaining the meaning of events.',
  wpm: 130,
  hookRule: 'A character already in trouble, mid-action. No setup, no context.',
  montageIntensity: 1,
  visual: 'Anamorphic feel, shallow depth of field, motivated light, blocking that places the character in relation to the space.'
};

const HORROR = {
  format: 'horror story',
  research: false,
  researchBrief: '',
  factSheetFraming: 'There is no fact sheet — this is fiction. Invent freely and keep the invented world internally consistent.',
  invention: 'Invention is REQUIRED. Never research, cite or hedge. The world is yours; its rules must stay consistent once set.',
  structure: '1) Normalcy, rendered in specific ordinary detail — the viewer must believe the place before it turns.\n2) The first wrongness: small, deniable, easy to explain away.\n3) Escalation — each chapter makes the previous explanation impossible.\n4) The thing is seen, or almost seen. Show less than the viewer wants.\n5) Aftermath, not resolution. Something remains.',
  voice: 'FIRST or close SECOND person, PRESENT tense. Short sentences; fragments allowed. Concrete sensory detail — sound, smell, temperature, texture — instead of descriptions of emotion. Never name the fear directly and never tell the viewer to be afraid. Withhold: the sentence that explains everything is the sentence that kills the dread.',
  wpm: 115,
  hookRule: 'A sensory wrongness, not a claim. One image or sound that is almost normal.',
  montageIntensity: 2,
  visual: 'Darkness used as composition rather than absence. Negative space, obscured figures, single practical light sources, frames held a beat too long.'
};

const PROFILES = {
  documentary: FACTUAL,
  educativ: Object.assign({}, FACTUAL, {
    format: 'explainer',
    structure: '1) A question the viewer can actually feel, not a definition.\n2) The simplest true answer.\n3) Why that answer is incomplete.\n4) The mechanism, one step at a time, each step depending only on what came before.\n5) What the viewer can now understand that they could not at the start.',
    voice: 'Second or third person, PRESENT tense. Define a term before using it. One new idea per beat. Analogies must be concrete and must not be stretched past their fit.',
    wpm: 140,
    hookRule: 'The everyday thing the viewer has never questioned, questioned.'
  }),
  conspiracy: Object.assign({}, FACTUAL, {
    format: 'investigative documentary',
    invention: 'You may report what people CLAIM, but you must NEVER assert an unverified claim as fact. Every contested statement is attributed to whoever makes it ("investigators alleged", "the family believed"). Inventing documents, quotes or numbers is forbidden. Where the record is thin, say the record is thin — that IS the story.',
    structure: '1) The official account, stated fairly and in full.\n2) The first DOCUMENTED detail that does not fit it.\n3) Each chapter adds one more verifiable discrepancy — never speculation stacked on speculation.\n4) Separate explicitly what is documented from what is inferred.\n5) Close on the strongest unanswered question, without asserting a conclusion the evidence does not support.',
    voice: 'Third person, PAST tense, scrupulously attributed. Never use loaded framing ("they don’t want you to know", "the truth they hid"). Trust the discrepancies to do the work.',
    wpm: 140,
    hookRule: 'The single documented discrepancy that is hardest to explain.'
  }),
  corporate: Object.assign({}, FACTUAL, {
    format: 'brand film',
    structure: '1) A problem the audience recognises as their own.\n2) Why the usual answers fall short.\n3) The approach, in plain terms.\n4) Concrete proof — numbers, named cases, results.\n5) One clear next step.',
    voice: 'First person plural or third person, PRESENT tense. Plain and confident, zero hype. Claims are specific and checkable. No superlative without a number behind it.',
    wpm: 140,
    hookRule: 'The problem stated in the audience’s own words.',
    visual: 'Clean, bright, shallow depth of field, real workplaces and real hands at work.'
  }),
  motivational: Object.assign({}, FACTUAL, {
    format: 'motivational piece',
    invention: 'Use only real examples; never invent a person’s story or attribute a quote that was not said.',
    structure: '1) Name the exact moment of resistance the viewer is in right now.\n2) Why the usual advice fails there.\n3) One reframe, stated once, plainly.\n4) A concrete action small enough to do today.\n5) Return to the opening moment, changed.',
    voice: 'SECOND person, PRESENT tense. Short declaratives. No lists, no "top five". Earn every imperative — at most one command per chapter.',
    wpm: 135,
    hookRule: 'The viewer’s own excuse, said back to them accurately.',
    montageIntensity: 2
  }),
  horror: HORROR,
  dark: HORROR,
  cinematic: FICTION,
  dramatic: Object.assign({}, FICTION, {
    format: 'drama',
    wpm: 125,
    hookRule: 'A relationship already breaking, caught mid-sentence.'
  }),
  emotional: Object.assign({}, FICTION, {
    format: 'emotional short film',
    wpm: 120,
    voice: FICTION.voice + ' Restraint carries the feeling: understate at the exact moment the viewer expects you to swell.',
    hookRule: 'A small, specific, ordinary detail that will matter enormously later.',
    montageIntensity: 0
  }),
  epic: Object.assign({}, FICTION, {
    format: 'epic narrative',
    wpm: 135,
    hookRule: 'The scale of what is at stake, shown through one person who cannot avoid it.',
    visual: 'Wide vistas against small figures, strong backlight, deep staging, weather as a character.',
    montageIntensity: 2
  })
};

let p = PROFILES[tone] || FACTUAL;
let source = 'built-in';

// Prefer the Airtable row when it exists and is coherent.
try {
  const rec = ($input.first().json.records || [])[0];
  const f = rec && rec.fields;
  if (f && f['Format'] && f['Structure'] && f['Voice']) {
    p = {
      format: f['Format'],
      research: f['Research'] === true,
      researchBrief: f['Research Brief'] || '',
      factSheetFraming: f['Fact Sheet Framing'] || '',
      invention: f['Invention'] || '',
      structure: f['Structure'],
      voice: f['Voice'],
      wpm: Number(f['WPM']) || p.wpm,
      hookRule: f['Hook Rule'] || p.hookRule,
      montageIntensity: Number.isFinite(Number(f['Montage Intensity'])) ? Number(f['Montage Intensity']) : p.montageIntensity,
      visual: f['Visual'] || p.visual,
    };
    source = 'airtable';
  }
} catch (e) { /* fetch failed — built-in profile stands */ }

return [{ json: { p: Object.assign({}, p, { tone: $('Receive Project Data').first().json.Tonalitate || 'Documentary', profileSource: source }) } }];

// Fiction genres skip research entirely — forcing a horror brief through a
// "verified facts are ground truth, do not invent" filter was exactly why
// every genre came out sounding like a documentary. The Story Bible prompt
// prints the genre's factSheetFraming on its own, so this branch only has
// to fill the notes slot with nothing.
return [{ json: { output: '' } }];

// Rebuilds, from the database alone, everything the in-loop regeneration
// holds in execution memory: the prompt, the aspect ratio, and the previous
// scene's image as a style reference. Images are generated ON Google Flow
// (useapi POST /google-flow/images), so a reference is a Flow media id —
// the previous scene's 'Image Media ID' — and no download/upload happens.
// Same body builder as Build Image Request in Media Generation; the MODEL
// string must agree with it and with Regenerate Scene Image.
const MODEL = 'nano-banana-2';
const scene = $('IR Load Scene').first().json;
const f = scene.fields || {};
const project = ($('IR Load Project').first().json || {}).fields || {};
const all = ($('IR Load Siblings').first().json.records || [])
  .slice()
  .sort((a, b) => (Number((a.fields || {})['Ordine Scenă']) || 0) - (Number((b.fields || {})['Ordine Scenă']) || 0));

let prompt = f['Imagine First Frame'] || f['Prompt Vizual'] || '';
if (!prompt) throw new Error('Scene ' + scene.id + ' has no image prompt.');
// Reviewer feedback steers the re-roll instead of repeating it blindly.
const note = String(f['Observații Scenă'] || '').trim();
if (note && !/REJECTED|FAILED|AUTO-REWRITE/i.test(note)) {
  prompt += '\n\nADJUSTMENT REQUEST — the new image MUST follow this: ' + note;
}
const rejectedBefore = /REJECTED|AUTO-REWRITE/i.test(note);

// The producer's uploaded reference photo is GROUND TRUTH for the film's
// first scene (Ordine 1) — it survives rejections too, because unlike the
// generated n-1 chain it is producer-approved content. The batch uploads it
// to Flow once per film and stores the id as refImageMediaId; a project
// whose batch predates that has only the Drive URL, which Flow cannot take
// as a reference, so the re-roll runs without it (logged, not fatal).
let opts = {};
try { opts = JSON.parse(project['Editing Options'] || '{}') || {}; } catch (e) {}
const isFirstScene = Number(f['Ordine Scenă']) === 1;
let userRefId = isFirstScene ? String(opts.refImageMediaId || '') : '';
if (isFirstScene && !userRefId && String(opts.refImage || '').startsWith('http')) {
  console.log('IR: scene 1 has a reference photo but no refImageMediaId yet (the next batch pass uploads it) — regenerating without it.');
}

// Previous scene's image = identity/wardrobe/palette reference, by media id.
// Same guard as the batch path: when consecutive prompts are largely the
// same words, drop it and let the text carry consistency. After a REJECTION
// the reference is dropped unconditionally — the model reproduces the
// refused content straight from the reference no matter how clean the new
// prompt is.
const idx = all.findIndex((r) => r.id === scene.id);
let prevId = '';
if (!userRefId && idx > 0 && !rejectedBefore) {
  const prev = all[idx - 1].fields || {};
  prevId = String(prev['Image Media ID'] || '');
  const wordSet = (s) => new Set(String(s || '').toLowerCase().replace(/[^\p{L}\p{N}\s]/gu, ' ').split(/\s+/).filter((w) => w.length > 3));
  const A = wordSet(prompt), B = wordSet(prev['Imagine First Frame']);
  if (A.size && B.size) {
    let hit = 0;
    for (const w of A) if (B.has(w)) hit++;
    if (hit / Math.max(A.size, B.size) > 0.55) prevId = '';
  }
}

const aspect = project['Format'] === '9:16' ? '9:16' : '16:9';
// count: 1 is mandatory (the API defaults to four); captchaRetry: 1 so a
// throttled account does not spend five captcha solves per request.
const body = { email: 'fermafabiz@gmail.com', model: MODEL, prompt: prompt, aspectRatio: aspect, count: 1, captchaRetry: 1 };
if (userRefId) {
  body.prompt = 'The reference image is GROUND TRUTH for this film: recreate its subject faithfully — the same exact appearance, design details, materials, colors and overall look — while composing the shot described here: ' + prompt;
  body.reference_1 = userRefId;
} else if (prevId) {
  body.prompt = 'Use the reference image ONLY for character identity, wardrobe, color palette and film look. Create a COMPLETELY NEW shot with a different composition, camera angle and framing — do NOT copy the reference layout: ' + prompt;
  body.reference_1 = prevId;
}
return [{ json: {
  sceneId: scene.id,
  prompt: prompt,
  requestBody: body,
  usedReference: userRefId || prevId || null,
  refIsUser: !!userRefId,
  aspect: aspect,
} }];

// Same voice rules as AB Pick Voice in Media Generation — chapters mode
// picks the chapter's narrator, characters mode splits the text into tagged
// segments, everything else keeps the project narrator.
const pf = ($('VR Load Project').first().json || {}).fields || {};
const pv = String(pf['Voice ID'] || '');
const fallback = pv.includes('_') ? pv : 'elevenlabs_hpp4J3VqNfWAUOO0d1Us';
let voice = fallback, mode = 'off', cast = [], assign = {}, chapterVoices = {};
try {
  const opts = JSON.parse(pf['Editing Options'] || '{}') || {};
  mode = String(opts.multiVoiceMode || 'off');
  cast = (Array.isArray(opts.cast) ? opts.cast : []).filter(v => typeof v === 'string' && v.includes('_'));
  assign = (opts.castAssign && typeof opts.castAssign === 'object') ? opts.castAssign : {};
  // Explicit per-chapter narrators picked in the audio panel; the key is
  // the chapter number as a string, plus 'hook' for the opening scene.
  chapterVoices = (opts.chapterVoices && typeof opts.chapterVoices === 'object') ? opts.chapterVoices : {};
} catch (e) {}
const f = $('VR Load Scene').first().json.fields || {};
const text = String(f['Script Scenă'] || '');
if (mode === 'chapters' && (cast.length || Object.keys(chapterVoices).length)) {
  const o = Number(f['Ordine Scenă']);
  const chapter = Number.isFinite(o) ? Math.floor(o / 100) : 0;
  // An explicit pick always wins — including for the hook, which has
  // no position in the cast and otherwise keeps the main narrator.
  const picked = chapterVoices[chapter > 0 ? String(chapter) : 'hook'];
  if (typeof picked === 'string' && picked.includes('_')) voice = picked;
  else if (chapter > 0 && cast.length) voice = cast[(chapter - 1) % cast.length];
}
let multi = false, segments = [];
if (mode === 'characters' && cast.length) {
  const all = (($('VR Load All Scenes').first().json || {}).records || [])
    .sort((a, b) => (Number((a.fields || {})['Ordine Scenă']) || 0) - (Number((b.fields || {})['Ordine Scenă']) || 0));
  const order = [];
  for (const sc of all) {
    const t = String((sc.fields || {})['Script Scenă'] || '');
    for (const m of t.matchAll(/\[\s*CHARACTER:\s*([^\]]+)\]/gi)) {
      const n = m[1].trim();
      if (n && !order.includes(n)) order.push(n);
    }
  }
  const voiceFor = (name) => {
    const a = assign[name];
    if (typeof a === 'string' && a.includes('_')) return a;
    const i = order.indexOf(name);
    return cast[(i >= 0 ? i : 0) % cast.length];
  };
  let cur = fallback;
  for (const tk of text.split(/(\[[^\[\]]{1,60}\])/)) {
    if (/^\[\s*NARRATOR\s*\]$/i.test(tk)) { cur = fallback; continue; }
    const cm = tk.match(/^\[\s*CHARACTER:\s*([^\]]+)\]$/i);
    if (cm) { cur = voiceFor(cm[1].trim()); continue; }
    const t = tk.replace(/\s+/g, ' ').trim();
    if (!t) continue;
    const last = segments[segments.length - 1];
    if (last && last.voice_id === cur) last.text += ' ' + t;
    else segments.push({ text: t, voice_id: cur });
  }
  multi = segments.length > 0 && segments.some(s => s.voice_id !== fallback);
}
return [{ json: { voice_id: voice, multi, segments } }];

// Dialogue instructions for the writing prompts. Empty strings in every
// mode except 'characters', so classic projects render the exact same
// prompt text as before this node existed.
let mode = 'off', cast = [], narration = 'with', category = '', catOpts = {};
try {
  const pf = ($('Fetch Project Record').first().json || {}).fields || {};
  const o = JSON.parse(pf['Editing Options'] || '{}') || {};
  mode = String(o.multiVoiceMode || 'off');
  cast = Array.isArray(o.cast) ? o.cast : [];
  narration = String((o.categoryOptions || {}).narration || 'with');
  category = String(o.category || '');
  catOpts = (o.categoryOptions && typeof o.categoryOptions === 'object') ? o.categoryOptions : {};
} catch (e) {}
// A silent film overrides every voice mode: nobody speaks at all.
const cinematic = category === 'cinematic';
const characters = !cinematic && mode === 'characters' && cast.length > 0;
const noNarrator = characters && narration === 'none';
const narrationRules = cinematic ? `\nSILENT FILM MODE — THIS FILM HAS NO NARRATION AND NO SPOKEN WORDS AT ALL (as important as the word count rule).\nThe text you write is a SILENT VISUAL BEAT SHEET: it is never spoken, never synthesized, never shown on screen. It exists only to drive the edit — each ~20-22 word beat becomes one 8-second shot, so the word count rule above still applies exactly.\nRules:\n(a) Terse, concrete, PRESENT-TENSE visual action — what the camera sees, one event per beat ("The black coupe snaps sideways into the hairpin, gravel spraying off the cliff edge").\n(b) No dialogue, no quotes, no inner thoughts, no addressing the viewer — nobody speaks in this film.\n(c) Every beat implies SOUND: engines, impacts, weather, footsteps, machinery — the film is carried entirely by the natural sound of what is on screen.\n(d) The story still escalates beat to beat like a real film: setup, build, payoff.\n` : !characters ? '' : noNarrator ? `\nDIALOGUE MODE — THIS FILM IS CHARACTER-DRIVEN, WITH NO NARRATOR AT ALL (as important as the word count rule).\nEvery single beat is SPOKEN by a Story Bible character; the tag [NARRATOR] is FORBIDDEN and must never appear. Rules:\n(a) Tag EVERY beat with its speaker at the start of the line, exactly: [CHARACTER: Name] — Name must match a Story Bible character name exactly. Never leave a line untagged.\n(b) Scene-setting, time jumps and transitions are carried by the characters themselves — what they say, notice, or react to — never by narration.\n(c) Dialogue is natural speech: contractions, hesitations, rhythm. Characters reveal the story by talking to each other; no exposition dumps, no character narrating facts at the camera.\n(d) Beats stay ~20-22 words (tags count as zero words); the total word count rule above still applies.\n(e) A character line is FIRST-PERSON SPEECH. Never write third-person narration inside a character tag — "he slides the stub into her notebook" is narration wearing a costume. If a beat cannot be said naturally by a character, rewrite it as something a character would actually say.\n` : `\nDIALOGUE MODE — THIS FILM IS CHARACTER-DRIVEN (as important as the word count rule).\nThe chapter is carried by the Story Bible characters SPEAKING to each other in direct dialogue. Rules:\n(a) 60-80% of the words are spoken lines; the narrator appears only in short connective beats (scene-setting, transitions), never to explain what dialogue could show.\n(b) Tag EVERY beat with its speaker at the start of the line, exactly: [NARRATOR] for narration, [CHARACTER: Name] for speech — Name must match a Story Bible character name exactly. Never leave a line untagged.\n(c) Dialogue is natural speech: contractions, hesitations, rhythm. Characters reveal the story by talking to each other; no exposition dumps, no character narrating facts at the camera.\n(d) Beats stay ~20-22 words (tags count as zero words); the total word count rule above still applies.\n`;
const segmentRules = cinematic ? `\nSILENT FILM MODE: narrator_text is a silent shot note — it is never spoken and never captioned — so keep it a terse description of the visible action. ALL storytelling lives in image_prompt and video_motion_prompt. In every video_motion_prompt make the action's SOUND read clearly from the visuals (a revving engine, screeching tires, pouring rain, a slamming door): the film's only audio is the natural sound of what is on screen.\n` : !characters ? '' : `\nDIALOGUE MODE: the chapter script contains speaker tags ${noNarrator ? '[CHARACTER: Name] (no narrator exists in this film)' : '[NARRATOR] and [CHARACTER: Name]'}. In narrator_text PRESERVE every tag verbatim: each copied line keeps its tag, and if you split a tagged passage across two scenes, repeat the tag at the start of the second part. A scene may contain several tagged lines. In every image prompt, put the SPEAKING character(s) on screen — paste their full visual_description — and match the shot to the exchange (over-the-shoulder for confrontations, close-up for whispered lines).\n`;
const hookRules = cinematic ? `\nTHIS FILM HAS NO NARRATION: the hook line is an ON-SCREEN TITLE only, never spoken. Write a short, punchy title of at most 7 words — no full sentence, no question aimed at the viewer by a narrator voice that does not exist.\n` : !noNarrator ? '' : `\nTHIS FILM HAS NO NARRATOR. The hook too is SPOKEN by a Story Bible character, in first person, and must start with its speaker tag exactly: [CHARACTER: Name] (Name matching the Story Bible). Never write untagged narration — untagged text would fall back to a narrator voice that does not exist in this film.\n`;
// KIDS STORY MODE is ADDITIVE: it composes with whatever voice mode is on
// (a silent kids film keeps the silent-film rules, a dialogue one keeps its
// tags) instead of replacing it, so the blocks below only ever APPEND.
// Non-kids projects append empty strings and render byte-identical prompts.
const kids = category === 'kids';
const kidsIllustrated = String(catOpts.visual_style || 'illustrated') !== 'cartoon3d';
const kidsStylePrefix = kidsIllustrated
  ? "Children's storybook illustration, soft watercolor and gouache textures, warm pastel palette, rounded friendly character shapes, gentle diffuse lighting"
  : 'High-quality 3D animated film still for children, soft rounded character design, expressive friendly faces, vivid warm colors, cinematic soft lighting';
const kidsNarration = !kids || cinematic ? '' : `
KIDS STORY MODE — this film is for young children (about ages 3-8) and every line is read aloud to them (as important as the word count rule).
(a) Short, simple sentences: one idea per sentence, everyday words a small child knows. No abstract vocabulary, no long clause chains.
(b) Warm and playful: the narrator is a storyteller by the bed, not a documentarian — gentle humour, wonder, and an occasional direct question to the little listener ("And do you know what the little fox did next?").
(c) Use repetition and rhythm the way picture books do: a recurring phrase or sound word children can anticipate and say along.
(d) NOTHING frightening: no death, no violence, no monsters played for fear, no peril without quick comfort. Tension stays mild and resolves kindly.
(e) The story still has a real arc — a lovable hero, a small problem, a warm ending with a gentle lesson worn lightly, never preached.
`;
const kidsSegment = !kids ? '' : `
KIDS STORY MODE — the film LOOKS like an animated children's film, never like photography.
(a) START every image_prompt with this exact style prefix, word for word, before anything else: "${kidsStylePrefix}". The style is the film's identity; a single photorealistic frame breaks it.
(b) Render every character and set description from the Story Bible IN that style: rounded, friendly proportions — no photorealistic humans anywhere in this film.
(c) In every video_motion_prompt keep movement gentle and easy to follow — a picture book coming softly to life, never fast or violent action.
`;
const kidsHook = !kids || cinematic || noNarrator ? '' : `
KIDS STORY: open like a storyteller inviting a child in — warm, curious, direct ("Have you ever wondered...?") — never dramatic or ominous.
`;
return [{ json: { mode, characters, noNarrator, cinematic, kids, castSize: cast.length, narrationRules: narrationRules + kidsNarration, segmentRules: segmentRules + kidsSegment, hookRules: hookRules + kidsHook } }];

const j = $input.first().json;
const s = (j.status || '').toLowerCase();
if (s === 'error') throw new Error('multi-voice TTS failed: ' + (j.error || 'unknown'));
if ($runIndex > 40) throw new Error('multi-voice TTS timed out after ' + $runIndex + ' polls. Last status: ' + s);
return [{ json: j }];

// Parse the research output into structured, sourced claims. Feeds three
// consumers: the Story Bible / narration / segmenter prompts (claimsList),
// the Evidence table (claims), and the thin-research flag on the project.
// The No Research branch flows through here too, yielding zero claims, so
// $('Extract Claims') is always safe to reference downstream.
const raw = String($json.output ?? '');
const researched = raw.trim() !== '' && !/^NO RESEARCH NEEDED/i.test(raw.trim());
const claims = [];
if (researched) {
  const re = /CLAIM:\s*(.+?)\s*\|\s*SOURCE:\s*(.+?)\s*\|\s*URL:\s*(\S+?)\s*(?:\|\s*DATE:\s*(.+?))?\s*$/gim;
  let m;
  while ((m = re.exec(raw)) !== null && claims.length < 20) {
    const url = m[3].replace(/[).,\]]+$/, '');
    // A claim without a resolvable URL is dropped, exactly as the prompt warns.
    if (!/^https?:\/\/\S+\.\S+/i.test(url)) continue;
    claims.push({
      ref: 'E' + (claims.length + 1),
      claim: m[1].trim(),
      source: m[2].trim(),
      url,
      date: (m[4] || '').trim() || 'n/a',
    });
  }
}
const notes = researched ? raw.replace(/^\s*CLAIMS:\s*$[\s\S]*/im, '').trim() : '';
const claimsList = claims
  .map((c) => `${c.ref}. ${c.claim} [${c.source} — ${c.url}${c.date !== 'n/a' ? ', ' + c.date : ''}]`)
  .join('\n');
// Under 6 claims on a researched topic = thin. The project gets flagged for
// manual review (Research Thin checkbox) but the script still proceeds.
const thin = researched && claims.length < 6;
const output = notes + (claimsList ? '\n\nSOURCED CLAIMS (ground truth, each with an ID):\n' + claimsList : '');
return [{ json: { output, notes, claims, claimsList, refs: claims.map((c) => c.ref), thin, researched } }];

// One Airtable create-batch (max 10 records) per output item. This node is
// IN-LINE on the way to Generate Story Bible (a canceled run used to lose
// its evidence because the parallel branch only flushed at the very end),
// so with zero claims it must still emit one passthrough item — returning
// [] here would stop the whole scripting chain for fiction projects.
const claims = $json.claims || [];
const pid = $('Receive Project Data').first().json.Project_ID;
const rows = claims.map((c) => ({
  fields: {
    'Claim': c.claim,
    'Ref': c.ref,
    'Source Name': c.source,
    'Source URL': c.url,
    'Source Date': c.date,
    'Project_ID': pid,
    'Proiect': [pid],
  },
}));
if (!rows.length) return [{ json: { records: [] } }];
const out = [];
for (let i = 0; i < rows.length; i += 10) out.push({ json: { records: rows.slice(i, i + 10) } });
return out;

// Evidence refs come from a model, so they are claims until proven: keep only
// refs that exist in the project's evidence pack, and flag scenes that made a
// factual claim the pack cannot back. With no research pack (fiction genres)
// every scene is forced to no-ref/unflagged.
const known = new Set($('Extract Claims').first().json.refs || []);
const items = $input.all();
// Under-delivery guard: Plan Scene Splits decided the scene count in code, so
// a shortfall here means the segmenter dropped narration on the floor (it did
// exactly that before the split moved into code). Nothing to auto-repair —
// the visuals only exist for the scenes it returned — but never let it pass
// unlogged the way it did in execution 877.
try {
  const planned = $('Plan Scene Splits').all().reduce((n, it) => n + (it.json.sceneCount || 0), 0);
  if (planned && items.length < planned) {
    console.log(`SCENE SHORTFALL: planned ${planned} scenes, segmenter returned ${items.length}. Narration was dropped.`);
  }
} catch (e) {}
for (const item of items) {
  const j = item.json;
  const required = j.evidence_required === true && known.size > 0;
  const cited = String(j.evidence_ref || '')
    .toUpperCase()
    .split(/[^E0-9]+/)
    .filter((r) => /^E\d+$/.test(r));
  const valid = [...new Set(cited)].filter((r) => known.has(r));
  j.evidence_required = required;
  j.evidence_ref = required ? valid.join(', ') : '';
  // Required but nothing real to cite -> the model invented or omitted its
  // source. The scene still ships; the flag routes it to human review.
  j.needs_fact_check = required && valid.length === 0;
}
return items;

// Save Evidence emits one item per 10-claim batch (and its Airtable
// response). The Story Bible prompt reads $json.output, so collapse back
// to the Extract Claims payload — one item, prompt untouched.
return [{ json: $('Extract Claims').first().json }];

// The segmenter used to be TOLD a scene count and trusted to distribute the
// narration itself. gpt-5.4 quietly under-delivered: a 96-word chapter that
// asked for 5 scenes came back as ONE scene holding the first sentence, and
// the other 83 words vanished from the film (execution 877). Prompts cannot
// enforce this — so the split happens HERE, in code, and the model only
// writes visuals for text it is handed verbatim.
const WORDS_PER_SCENE = 22; // one 8-second beat
// The most a single scene may carry. A fold has no ceiling of its own — a
// runt merged into a full chunk simply becomes a long chunk — and the film
// pays for that at the very end of the pipeline, where the clip is stretched
// to the narration: on the 71-scene Boyd film one chunk came out at 34 words,
// which is 16.7 seconds of voice over an 8-second clip, and the assembler
// could only cover it with slow motion and then a frozen frame. 1.45x the
// target leaves the natural 27-28 word scenes alone and cuts only outliers.
const MAX_WORDS_PER_SCENE = Math.round(WORDS_PER_SCENE * 1.45);
const wc = (s) => String(s || '').trim().split(/\s+/).filter(Boolean).length;

/**
 * Cut one span of text in two, as near its middle as a real boundary allows.
 *
 * `where` names the coarsest boundary to accept: a sentence end if there is
 * one, then clause punctuation, then a plain word gap for a run-on with no
 * punctuation at all. One owner, because both the unit splitter above and the
 * ceiling below need exactly this and a second copy would drift.
 */
function cutNear(u, where = 'sentence') {
  const res =
    where === 'word'
      ? [/\s+/g]
      : where === 'clause'
        ? [/[,;:—]\s+/g, /\s+/g]
        : [/(?<=[.!?]["”’')]*)\s+/g, /[,;:—]\s+/g, /\s+/g];
  for (const re of res) {
    const pos = [];
    let m;
    re.lastIndex = 0;
    while ((m = re.exec(u)) !== null) pos.push(m.index + m[0].length);
    if (!pos.length) continue;
    const mid = Math.floor(u.length / 2);
    let best = pos[0];
    for (const p of pos) if (Math.abs(p - mid) < Math.abs(best - mid)) best = p;
    const a = u.slice(0, best).trim();
    const b = u.slice(best).trim();
    if (a && b) return [a, b];
  }
  return null;
}

function planChunks(raw) {
  const text = String(raw || '').replace(/\s+/g, ' ').trim();
  const total = wc(text);
  if (!total) return [];
  let target = Math.max(1, Math.ceil(total / WORDS_PER_SCENE));
  let units = (text.match(/[^.!?]+(?:[.!?]+["”’')]*|$)/g) || [text])
    .map((s) => s.trim())
    .filter(Boolean);
  if (!units.length) units = [text];
  let ideal = total / target;
  // Cut over-long units: first to REACH the target count, then to stop any
  // single unit dwarfing the rest. Clause punctuation preferred; a run-on
  // with no punctuation at all falls back to a word boundary.
  for (let guard = 0; guard < 80; guard++) {
    const need = units.length < target;
    let bi = -1, bw = 0, byClause = true;
    for (let k = 0; k < units.length; k++) {
      const w = wc(units[k]);
      if (w <= bw) continue;
      const has = /[,;:—]\s/.test(units[k]);
      if (!has && w < ideal * 1.8) continue;
      if (!need && w <= ideal * 1.5) continue;
      bw = w; bi = k; byClause = has;
    }
    if (bi < 0) break;
    const cut = cutNear(units[bi], byClause ? 'clause' : 'word');
    if (!cut) break;
    units.splice(bi, 1, cut[0], cut[1]);
  }
  target = Math.min(target, units.length);
  const chunks = [];
  let idx = 0, rem = total;
  for (let c = 0; c < target; c++) {
    const remC = target - c;
    const maxTake = units.length - idx - (remC - 1);
    const want = rem / remC;
    let take = 1, w = wc(units[idx]);
    while (take < maxTake) {
      const nxt = wc(units[idx + take]);
      if (w >= want * 0.75 || w + nxt > want * 1.45) break;
      w += nxt; take++;
    }
    chunks.push(units.slice(idx, idx + take).join(' '));
    idx += take; rem -= w;
  }
  if (idx < units.length) chunks[chunks.length - 1] += ' ' + units.slice(idx).join(' ');
  // An 8-second shot carrying four words is dead air — fold runts away.
  const floor = Math.max(6, (total / chunks.length) * 0.4);
  let k = 0;
  while (chunks.length > 1 && k < chunks.length) {
    if (wc(chunks[k]) < floor) {
      const j = (k > 0 && (k === chunks.length - 1 || wc(chunks[k - 1]) <= wc(chunks[k + 1]))) ? k - 1 : k + 1;
      const lo = Math.min(k, j), hi = Math.max(k, j);
      chunks.splice(lo, hi - lo + 1, chunks[lo] + ' ' + chunks[hi]);
      k = 0;
    } else k++;
  }
  // …and now the other end of the same rule. Folding a runt away can only
  // make a chunk bigger, and nothing above ever checked how big — so this
  // splits anything over the ceiling back down, at a sentence boundary where
  // there is one. Bounded, because a chunk with no boundary inside it cannot
  // be cut and must be allowed to stand.
  for (let guard = 0; guard < 40; guard++) {
    const over = chunks.findIndex((c) => wc(c) > MAX_WORDS_PER_SCENE);
    if (over < 0) break;
    const cut = cutNear(chunks[over]);
    if (!cut) break;
    chunks.splice(over, 1, cut[0], cut[1]);
  }
  return chunks;
}

const out = [];
const items = $input.all();
for (let i = 0; i < items.length; i++) {
  const j = items[i].json;
  const chunks = planChunks(((j.fields || {})['Script Capitol']));
  const safe = chunks.length ? chunks : [String(((j.fields || {})['Script Capitol']) || '').trim()];
  console.log(`Chapter ${(j.fields || {})['Ordine']}: ${wc(((j.fields || {})['Script Capitol']))} words -> ${safe.length} scenes`);
  out.push({
    json: Object.assign({}, j, {
      sceneCount: safe.length,
      sceneChunks: safe,
      chunkList: safe.map((c, n) => `${n + 1}. ${c}`).join('\n'),
    }),
    // Save scenes To Airtable1 resolves the chapter record through
    // $('Create Chapter Records').item — keep the pairing intact.
    pairedItem: { item: i },
  });
}
return out;

// One item out, or the agent below runs once per scene.
//
// The scene list is taken from Validate Evidence Refs — the last node that has
// every scene in the order the film plays — and each scene is handed to the
// model with the SHOT and MOTION prompts beside its narration. Those two are
// the load-bearing half of the input: they are what lets the model see that a
// scene is already showing the thing it was about to draw, which is the
// mistake this whole feature was born from.
const items = $('Validate Evidence Refs').all();
const scenes = items.map((it, i) => {
  const j = it.json || {};
  const chapter = Number(j.chapter_number ?? 0);
  const scene = Number(j.scene_number ?? i + 1);
  return {
    index: i,
    // chapter*100 + scene, the convention every workflow already shares.
    order: chapter * 100 + scene,
    chapter,
    narration: String(j.narrator_text || ''),
    shot: String(j.image_prompt || ''),
    motion: String(j.video_motion_prompt || ''),
  };
});

let research = '';
try { research = String($('Extract Claims').first().json.output || ''); } catch (e) {}

let title = '';
let tone = '';
try {
  const p = $('Receive Project Data').first().json || {};
  title = String(p['Nume Proiect'] || p.name || p.Tema || '');
  tone = String(p.Tonalitate || p.tone || '');
} catch (e) {}

const brief = [
  `FILM: ${title}`,
  `TONE: ${tone}`,
  '',
  'SCENES (index · narration · what the shot will show)',
  ...scenes.map((s) => [
    `${s.index} · ${s.narration}`,
    `    SHOT: ${s.shot}`,
    `    MOTION: ${s.motion}`,
  ].join('\n')),
  '',
  'RESEARCH PACK (may be empty)',
  research || '(none — this film was not researched, so no fact from outside the script is available)',
].join('\n');

return [{ json: { brief, scenes, sceneCount: scenes.length } }];

